WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.973
Start with two application workers behind a load
balancer. A visitor edits a page through

00:00:04.973 --> 00:00:10.114
worker A and then reads it through worker B. If
the edit exists only in A's memory, B cannot

00:00:10.114 --> 00:00:11.287
produce the new page.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.830
Sticky routing can hide that problem until A
fails, but it does not create a shared, durable

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:21.929
record. Moving page state into a database lets
either worker load the same authoritative

00:00:21.929 --> 00:00:26.969
information. The application becomes replaceable
because its process lifetime no longer

00:00:26.969 --> 00:00:29.113
determines the lifetime of user data.

00:00:29.113 --> 00:00:34.151
This is why adding stateless workers is
straightforward, while adding independent copies
of

00:00:34.151 --> 00:00:36.809
a database is a different architectural problem.

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:42.045
The database now carries two responsibilities:
persistence and coordination. Persistence

00:00:42.045 --> 00:00:47.520
keeps an accepted edit after the application
stops. Coordination makes concurrent operations

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.995
agree on shared facts, such as which page
revision is current. Draw arrows from both
workers

00:00:52.995 --> 00:00:57.815
into one database: the drawing explains both the
benefit and the next bottleneck.

00:00:57.815 --> 00:01:03.313
Every worker can consult a common authority, but
all their storage operations also converge

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:08.691
there. A load balancer normally performs
relatively little work per request; the database

00:01:08.691 --> 00:01:13.343
may locate rows, check constraints, update
indexes and write durable records.

00:01:13.343 --> 00:01:18.030
Increasing the worker count therefore does not
automatically increase the capacity of the

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:18.715
whole system.

00:01:18.843 --> 00:01:23.926
Consider a staff directory containing people,
departments and buildings. Each person refers

00:01:23.926 --> 00:01:29.009
to one department, and each department refers to
its building. Keeping the building address

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:33.086
in one row means an address correction has one
authoritative destination.

00:01:33.086 --> 00:01:38.198
A person can also have an arbitrary number of
positions without inventing position1,

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:43.736
position2 and position3 columns: store positions
as separate rows connected by identifiers.

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:48.118
These references are the reason a normalized
schema uses several tables.

00:01:48.118 --> 00:01:52.942
The final page may look like one object, but its
facts have different owners and different

00:01:52.942 --> 00:01:57.821
repetition patterns. A join reconstructs the
view that a particular screen needs from those

00:01:57.821 --> 00:01:59.322
separately maintained facts.

00:01:59.322 --> 00:02:04.717
Follow the arrows in a directory query: select
the person, locate the department using the

00:02:04.717 --> 00:02:10.113
referenced identifier, and then find the
building. SQL describes the desired result
rather

00:02:10.113 --> 00:02:15.568
than requiring the application to prescribe
every lookup. The database chooses an execution

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:15.868
plan.

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:21.151
Suppose a different query asks for recent posts
written by users over a hundred years old.

00:02:21.151 --> 00:02:26.258
Starting with the tiny set of qualifying users
can avoid inspecting a large fraction of

00:02:26.258 --> 00:02:31.012
recent posts; starting with recent posts can
produce much more intermediate work.

00:02:31.012 --> 00:02:36.447
Both plans can return the same answer. Their
cost depends on selectivity, available indexes

00:02:36.447 --> 00:02:41.106
and the actual distribution of data, not merely
on the number of tables named.

00:02:41.106 --> 00:02:46.238
A product catalog can be ordered by price, but
that arrangement does not also order it by

00:02:46.238 --> 00:02:51.543
manufacturer or product name. Scanning the
entire catalog for each alternative lookup
wastes

00:02:51.543 --> 00:02:56.791
work. An index supplies a separate search
structure containing values and references to
the

00:02:56.791 --> 00:02:57.887
corresponding rows.

00:02:58.106 --> 00:03:03.214
The printed-catalog analogy is useful: the
products need not move when a reader switches

00:03:03.214 --> 00:03:08.497
from a manufacturer index to a name index. A
database goes further because its indexes must

00:03:08.497 --> 00:03:11.399
support insertions and deletions during
operation.

00:03:11.606 --> 00:03:16.261
Balanced tree structures can preserve efficient
lookup without rewriting half the data to

00:03:16.261 --> 00:03:19.296
make room for every new entry in a physically
sorted list.

00:03:19.296 --> 00:03:24.497
Now consider a directory query for a particular
first name and last name. Two independent

00:03:24.497 --> 00:03:29.523
indexes can locate either set of matches, but
the database may still need to filter or

00:03:29.523 --> 00:03:34.899
combine those sets. A composite index on first
name followed by last name organizes the pair

00:03:34.899 --> 00:03:35.425
together.

00:03:35.425 --> 00:03:40.590
Its ordering also explains a limitation: it can
support lookups beginning with the first-

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:45.812
name component, while a last-name-only lookup
generally cannot use that prefix in the same

00:03:45.812 --> 00:03:50.628
direct way. Index design follows actual query
predicates and ordering requirements.

00:03:50.628 --> 00:03:55.232
The diagram should show the query entering a
small search structure and then following a

00:03:55.232 --> 00:03:58.999
pointer to selected rows, rather than pretending
that every row is read.
